      <memberdef kind="variable" id="structerror_1a309e0f9558c08b1371d33cc1b6804cc0" prot="public" static="no" mutable="no">
        <type>struct <ref refid="structerror" kindref="compound">error</ref> *</type>
        <definition>struct error* error::cause</definition>
        <argsstring></argsstring>
        <name>cause</name>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Link to the cause and effect of given error. </para>        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The cause creates the effect: e1 = box.error.new({code = 0, reason = &apos;e1&apos;}) e2 = box.error.new({code = 0, reason = &apos;e2&apos;}) e1:set_prev(e2) <ndash/> Now e2 is the cause of e1 and e1 is the effect of e2. Only cause keeps reference to avoid cyclic dependence. RLIST implementation is not really suitable here since it is organized as circular list. In such a case it is impossible to start an iteration from any node and finish at the logical end of the list. Double-linked list is required to allow deletion from the middle of the list. </para>        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="/home/tsafin/tarantool/src/lib/core/diag.h" line="111" column="1" bodyfile="/home/tsafin/tarantool/src/lib/core/diag.h" bodystart="111" bodyend="-1"/>
      </memberdef>
